  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1056 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA dreary Halloween is ongoing across the Mid-South with showers\ncontinuing to push across the area. While there have been a few\npockets of heavier rainfall, instability has remained lacking\nwith no lightning activity on the latest GLM products. Shower\nactivity will continue through the overnight period and into\nSunday, with coverage likely not decreasing by much until the\nafternoon to evening. A few CAMs want to bring some elevated\ninstability up the MS River early Sunday morning, so a rumble or\ntwo of thunder cannot necessarily be ruled out mainly across\nnorth MS. This instability, if it materializes at all, will be\nshunted by midday with showers remaining. With the persistent\ncloud coverage and showers, expect high temperatures to span the\n60s with some locations reaching the 70s across eastern AR and\nnorthwest MS.\n\nBy Monday, an upper-level trough will continue to develop across\nthe Rockies with surface, southerly flow across the Mid-South.\nWhile conditions are expected to dry out, we will get ample WAA\nto the area with dew points rising back into the upper 60s. High\ntemperatures will be a few degrees above normal for the beginning\nof November, generally in the low to mid 70s. Into Tuesday, eyes\nwill be to the west as the aforementioned trough moves out of the\nRockies and deepens through the Plains. This system will bring\nour next chance for widespread showers and thunderstorms by\nTuesday evening into Wednesday. Timing of this system has slowed\ndown a bit for our area, with the bulk of the precipitation\narriving late Tuesday into early Wednesday. Overall, the severe\nweather set-up for our area does appear to be lacking with this\nsystem as instability will likely remain limited. Latest LREF\nprobabilities of favorable CAPE and shear levels are less than\n10%, with the more favorable conditions remaining to our west and\nsouth. We are still just on the outside of beginning to get some\nCAMs into range, so we will see what those bring to the forecast.\nOverall, this looks to be a rainfall event for the Mid-South at\nthis point with much of the area likely receiving 1 to 2\" of\nrainfall. Latest NBM guidance does show some swaths of about a\n30 to 40% chance of exceeding 2\" with the midweek system,\nalthough the location will likely vary over the coming days.\nAdditionally, NAEFS does have PWAT values over the 90th\npercentile Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\nBeyond Wednesday, the ultimate progression of the upper-level\ntrough and positioning of the parent low will come into play with\nhow quickly precipitation moves out. LREF guidance does have some\nvariability with our temperatures as a result, with one cluster\nfavoring a steady cool down back to normal versus another cluster\nportraying a sharper cool down by Thursday. Nonetheless, guidance\ndoes favor dry conditions returning to the Mid-South with near-\nnormal temperatures as we head into next weekend.",
